{"name":"Microwave Blue","hex":"#2d5254","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"microwave","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An electromagnetic wave with wavelength between that of infrared light and radio waves.","Ellipsis of microwave oven."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cook (something) in a microwave oven.","To provide minimal preparation for implementation, deeming full or extensive preparation to be unnecessary. (Can we add an example for this sense?)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/microwave"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Microwave","blocks":[{"text":"Microwave is a form of electromagnetic radiation with wavelengths shorter than other radio waves but longer than infrared waves. Its wavelength ranges from about one meter to one millimeter, corresponding to frequencies between 300 MHz and 300 GHz, broadly construed. A more common definition in radio-frequency engineering is the range between 1 and 100 GHz, or between 1 and 3000 GHz. In all cases, microwaves include the entire super high frequency (SHF) band at minimum. The boundaries between far infrared, terahertz radiation, microwaves, and ultra-high-frequency (UHF) are fairly arbitrary and differ between different fields of study."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Frazier_Peak%2C_tower_and_Honda_Element.jpg/330px-Frazier_Peak%2C_tower_and_Honda_Element.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microwave","wikidata":"Q127995"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Diplexer1.jpg/500px-Diplexer1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Diplexer1.jpg/500px-Diplexer1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Waveguide is used to carry microwaves. Example of waveguides and a diplexer in an air traffic control radar.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiplexer1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Magnetron_section_transverse_to_axis.JPG/500px-Magnetron_section_transverse_to_axis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Magnetron_section_transverse_to_axis.JPG/1280px-Magnetron_section_transverse_to_axis.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Magnetron_section_trans